Is NetHack a "role playing game"?

The spoiler information below suggests that if you
_don't_ adjust your play specifically to your role,
you are going to miss out on goodies, in some cases
lots of them, that might be appropriate to your
role, and help you win the game.

Who knew, for example, that priests could BUC ID
using the ";" farlook command, thus saving turns,
and nutrition, otherwise wasted to walk over and
stomp stuff, or were unusually good kickers?

If you play a priest like you play every other role,
then, you are diminishing your chances to win.

xanthian.

     This even all together, still isn't nearly as
     much fun, though, as was playing an INT:3 Dwarf,
     who was constantly getting into trouble by
     (player-deliberately) doing (hilariously) stupid
     stuff, in a tabletop AD&D game long ago.

     What does that tell you? That "Role Playing
     Game" is a matter of degree, valued on a
     continuum, it is not a binary valued datum, as
     nonsensical quarrels here would imply.

